$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 12/Claim type

::EPI-W12-001::'Hypertension prevalence is 28%' is primarily what type of claim? {
=Descriptive#Correct.
~Causal#Review the appraisal question before choosing an interpretation.
~Predictive#Review the appraisal question before choosing an interpretation.
~Experimental#Review the appraisal question before choosing an interpretation.
}

::EPI-W12-002::'Programme receipt was associated with lower mortality' is primarily what type of claim? {
=Associational#Correct.
~Purely descriptive#Review the appraisal question before choosing an interpretation.
~Necessarily causal#Review the appraisal question before choosing an interpretation.
~A diagnostic-accuracy claim#Review the appraisal question before choosing an interpretation.
}

$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 12/Design and temporality

::EPI-W12-003::Why should critical appraisal begin with design? {
=Design determines how participants, exposure, comparison and outcome were generated#Correct.
~P values determine design quality#Review the appraisal question before choosing an interpretation.
~Large samples make design irrelevant#Review the appraisal question before choosing an interpretation.
~Only software choice matters#Review the appraisal question before choosing an interpretation.
}

$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 12/Bias and confounding

::EPI-W12-004::Exposure was self-selected and participants were wealthier than non-participants. What is the most important appraisal point? {
=Residual confounding or selection may explain some association#Correct.
~The p-value removes this concern#Identify the plausible mechanism that could change interpretation.
~The study is automatically invalid in every respect#Identify the plausible mechanism that could change interpretation.
~No denominator is needed#Identify the plausible mechanism that could change interpretation.
}

::EPI-W12-005::Exposure and outcome were measured at the same visit. What is the most important appraisal point? {
=Temporal order may be ambiguous#Correct.
~The p-value removes this concern#Identify the plausible mechanism that could change interpretation.
~The study is automatically invalid in every respect#Identify the plausible mechanism that could change interpretation.
~No denominator is needed#Identify the plausible mechanism that could change interpretation.
}

::EPI-W12-006::Loss to follow-up was 15% in one group and 3% in the other. What is the most important appraisal point? {
=Differential attrition may introduce selection bias#Correct.
~The p-value removes this concern#Identify the plausible mechanism that could change interpretation.
~The study is automatically invalid in every respect#Identify the plausible mechanism that could change interpretation.
~No denominator is needed#Identify the plausible mechanism that could change interpretation.
}

::EPI-W12-007::Outcome assessors knew treatment status for a subjective outcome. What is the most important appraisal point? {
=Differential outcome measurement may introduce information bias#Correct.
~The p-value removes this concern#Identify the plausible mechanism that could change interpretation.
~The study is automatically invalid in every respect#Identify the plausible mechanism that could change interpretation.
~No denominator is needed#Identify the plausible mechanism that could change interpretation.
}

